# family: interest
# bridge: B(law-restatement)
# regime: exact (rows are packed peer bits, transitions are integers)
# instrument-first: 75bdb091
# baseline: tests/unit/interest, tests/integration/interest
# baseline-count: 54
# successor-form: matrix
#
# The unit is the verdict core, which is now NetwInterestEngine: the layer admit
# masks, the per-entity desired rows in ancestry order, the XOR delta and its
# ordering, the committed matrix, and the explainer over it.
#
# THE SPLIT WAS ALREADY PAID, which is what makes this batch unlike the four
# before it. Liveness, clock, session and scenes each began by carving a
# portable plane out of a file that mixed two. Interest did not: the core was
# already four files holding pure mask algebra with zero Object reach, and a
# test grepped its own source for thirteen forbidden tokens to keep it that way.
# So this crossing is a translation, and its risk sits entirely in what the new
# representation can do that the old one could not.
#
# THE KEY IS THE ONE REPRESENTATION CHANGE, AND RID WAS THE WRONG HANDLE. The
# schedule called for re-keying entity identity onto NetwEntity.rid. That is
# refused on measured evidence: rid is unset until liveness admits an entity and
# is cleared again at death (liveness_shell.gd sets RID() in three places), so
# several live entities hold an invalid one at the same time and keying on it
# would merge them. An off-tree entity holding an admission before it has any
# identity is the case that names it. The engine keys on an int64 slot minted by
# InterestCore instead, exactly as that interface already mints peer bits, and
# the slot is stable for as long as interest cares about the entity.
#
# TWO HAZARDS THE OLD SHAPE COULD NOT HAVE, both stated natively rather than
# discovered later. Zero is what set_parent spells "no parent" with, so an
# entity stored at slot zero could never clamp anybody and never be clamped, and
# every mutation now refuses it. And two entities can share a (depth, route)
# while the table holding them has no insertion order to fall back on, so the
# tie is broken on the slot and the transition order is total rather than
# usually-total.
#
# THE INSTRUMENT WAS RECORDED AND THEN RETIRED, and that is a deliberate choice
# rather than an omission. tests/crossing/interest_arm.gd and a 47-row golden
# were recorded at 75bdb091 before a line of the core was native, and red-proven
# eight times across eight independent halves: the layer mask's live clamp, the
# parent clamp, the hide ordering, the commit revision guard, the default layer
# policy, membership composition, the intent flag, and removal from the matrix.
# The first of those eight found a real hole in the arm, which then grew the
# case that closes it.
#
# What the arm is NOT is a second implementation of the specification, and the
# core's specification is already a set of properties rather than a script of
# observations. So the native side states the properties directly. The arm and
# its golden are deleted with the classes they drove, and the evidence they
# produced lives at 75bdb091 rather than in a file nothing reads. A golden with
# no reader is coverage that is always green because nobody looked.
#
# WHAT THE ROWS BELOW ARE CHECKED BY. The fifteen ported cases become fifteen
# native cases in interest_engine_tests.cpp. The thirty-nine that carry are not
# a hedge: every one of them drives a node, a handle, a signal or a live
# session through InterestCore, and all thirty-nine now run against the native
# engine, which is what makes the translation observable end to end rather than
# only in the tier that owns it.
#
# THE PURITY LINT COULD NOT CROSS AND IS NOT PRETENDING TO. It read the engine's
# own .gd source as a string and asserted thirteen tokens were absent. That file
# is gone, so the case would pass by reading nothing, which is the exact failure
# the census exists to catch. Its successor is structural: interest_engine.cpp
# includes no engine header that could reach a tree, a clock or a signal, and
# tools/check_sources.py holds every source to compiling.
#
# THE VANISHED-KEY COUNTER HAS NO PUBLIC PATH TO IT, found while writing the
# instrument. Removing an entity takes it out of the dirty set, and nothing else
# can leave a dirty key with no record, so the only case that ever raised that
# counter wrote the private set directly. GDScript cannot write a native
# private, so the case crosses to the tier where the member is reachable.
#
# CARRIED DEBT, named with its trigger. InterestCore holds two maps to translate
# NetwEntity into a slot and back. They collapse to one when NetwEntity itself
# is native and can carry its slot, which the shell swap schedules.
#
test_interest_engine.gd:test_l1_determinism_returns_identical_rows          -> interest_engine_tests.cpp:"[Networked][Interest][Hosted] the same state yields the same rows"
test_interest_engine.gd:test_l2_confluence_ignores_mutation_order           -> interest_engine_tests.cpp:"[Networked][Interest][Hosted] mutation order does not move the matrix"
